-- title:
--   Every constant value of a transcendental element is attained at some place
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $M$ be fields with $M$ a $K$-algebra, let $t \in M$ be transcendental over $K$, and assume that $M$ is finite-dimensional and separable over the intermediate field $K\langle t\rangle = K\!\left(t\right)$ generated by $t$. Let $c \in K$. Then there exists a place $v$ of $M$ over $K$ in the sense of the project's structure [`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22), that is, a valuation subring $v$ of $M$ which contains $\operatorname{algebraMap} K M\,a$ for every $a \in K$, is not all of $M$, and is a principal ideal ring, such that $0 < v.\mathrm{ord}\,(t - \operatorname{algebraMap} K M\,c)$. Here $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is the integer-valued order function $f \mapsto -\log$ of the value of the adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of the valuation ring, so the conclusion says that $t - c$ has strictly positive order, i.e. vanishes, at the place $v$.
--
--   This is the statement that a transcendental element $t$ of a function field $M/K$ attains each constant value $c \in K$ at some place, equivalently that the fibre of the restriction map from places of $M$ to places of $K(t)$ above the place $t = c$ is non-empty. It is used in the project's work on semistable coverings, for instance in the construction of elements of Riemann–Roch spaces with prescribed residues and of functions with prescribed order divisor.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Place_exists_ord_sub_algebraMap_pos_of_transcendental.lean

